lto-enabled-list (1.0deepin2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Remove gcc-12 and python3.12 from the list (619 -> 617 entries).
  * Both already build with LTO (and PGO) through their own packaging
    rules, so the vendor optimize/lto injection adds nothing for
    them: gcc-12 hardcodes the bootstrap stage flags (bootstrap-lto
    + profiled bootstrap) and the injected flags never reach the
    compiler; python3.12 sets with_lto/with_pgo itself on
    upstream-supported architectures, and on loong64/sw64 its rules
    enable neither, so injection would be an untested first LTO
    build there.

 -- tunaichao <tunaichao@uniontech.com>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 10:49:29 +0800

lto-enabled-list (1.0deepin1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial UOS package. The vendor build feature optimize/lto now
    follows an opt-in model: only the source packages listed here
    are built with LTO by default, every other package stays
    unchanged unless its own packaging opts in explicitly.
  * Initial population: the update-lto-20260910 rebuild campaign
    (deepin-community project 818) — 619 source packages with a
    merged "Rebuild with LTO enabled" pull request (618 original
    batch plus a 12-package supplementary batch from 2026-09-12),
    tagged any. Not listed: the four packages later confirmed
    broken (boost1.83, kwin, lapack, openexr), and binutils,
    cdrkit, gnustep-make, libnfs, x264, whose debian/rules hardcode
    CFLAGS so the injected LTO flags never reach the compiler.

 -- tunaichao <tunaichao@uniontech.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 16:20:00 +0800
